#  .gitattributes for pwiz

# Line endings. Git stores LF for every file it recognises as text and converts on
# checkout to the platform ending: core.autocrlf=true (or core.eol=native) gives CRLF on
# Windows, LF on Linux and macOS. Storage used to be a per-file coin flip (about 45% of .cs
# stored CRLF, the rest LF, some files mixed), so an LF write into a CRLF-stored file turned
# a one-line edit into a whole-file diff; see issue #4604. Everything below this line is an
# exception to text=auto, so keep the exceptions after it.
#
# Do NOT pin text test fixtures with -text to "keep them byte-exact": a -text file checks
# out LF on Windows too, which changes what every developer and Windows agent has always
# seen (CRLF), and breaks fixtures whose companions record a hash of the CRLF bytes (a .sky
# with its .skyl audit log, for example - TestFilesTreeForm). text=auto reproduces the old
# Windows bytes exactly; only Linux checkouts of formerly CRLF-stored fixtures change.
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf

# cmd.exe tracks its position in a batch file by byte offset, and that accounting
# drifts on an LF-only file: after a few `call :label` returns the search starts
# from the wrong place and fails with "The system cannot find the batch label
# specified". build.bat lost its entire `call :build_one` loop that way - it
# staged whatever binaries already existed and reported success. Checkouts with
# core.autocrlf=true happened to be immune, which is why CI never saw it.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Don't change line endings on potentially indexed mass spec data. Both spellings:
# attribute patterns are case-sensitive on Linux agents (core.ignorecase=false).
*.mzml binary
*.mzML binary
*.mzxml binary
*.mzXML binary

# Fixtures copied verbatim out of the cpp tests are reference data, not source: the whole point
# is that they are the same bytes pwiz C++ parses, so line endings must survive a checkout
# unchanged on every platform.
pwiz/**/test/TestData/** -text
